title: Okta / LAPSUS$
page: breaches/okta-lapsus-sitel.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Between January 16 and 21, 2022, LAPSUS$ had access to the environment of Sitel, a support subcontractor to Okta; customers learned in March, from screenshots LAPSUS$ posted.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 5 | days of access to Sitel's environment | January 16 to 21, 2022
stat: 2 | months before customers were told | and only because LAPSUS$ posted screenshots
stat: 366 | customers potentially affected | Okta's initial assessment: up to 366, about 2.5% of its base
stat: 4th | party | Sitel, a subcontractor providing customer support services to Okta
Okta's final report found two active customer tenants accessed, in 25 minutes of control of one Sitel workstation on January 21.
notes:
Start with the four figures the page leads with.

Between January 16 and 21, 2022, LAPSUS$ had access to the environment of Sitel, a subcontractor providing customer support services to Okta, through a support engineer's laptop. That is the five days.

The two months is how long it was before customers were told, and the page says they were told only because LAPSUS$ posted.

The 366 is Okta's initial assessment: up to 366 customers, around 2.5% of its base, may have been affected. Keep the words up to and may have been when you say it. The final forensic report Okta commissioned, whose conclusions Okta published in April 2022, found the attacker had controlled one Sitel workstation for 25 minutes on January 21 and accessed two active customer tenants. A later slide sets the two findings side by side.

A fourth party is a vendor's vendor. The page's tile names Sitel, a subcontractor providing customer support services to Okta. Okta is an identity provider, or IdP: the system that authenticates users.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: January 16 to 21, 2022 | LAPSUS$ has access to Sitel's environment, through a support engineer's laptop
event: January 21 | Per Okta's final report, one Sitel workstation is controlled for 25 minutes
event: March 17 | Okta receives a summary report from Sitel
event: March 22 | LAPSUS$ posts screenshots to Telegram, and customers find out
event: March 22, later | Okta receives the complete forensic report from Sitel
event: April 2022 | Okta publishes the conclusions of the final forensic report it commissioned
Okta's final report also found two active customer tenants accessed. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those.

LAPSUS$ had access to Sitel's environment between January 16 and 21, 2022, through a support engineer's laptop. The final forensic report Okta commissioned found the attacker had controlled one Sitel workstation for 25 minutes on January 21, and accessed two active customer tenants.

Okta received a summary report from Sitel on March 17. Customers found out on March 22, when LAPSUS$ posted screenshots to Telegram. Okta's security team correlated them to the January incident within ninety minutes of seeing them, and received the complete forensic report from Sitel later that same day. In April 2022, Okta published the conclusions of the final forensic report it commissioned.

For two months, the organizations whose identity provider had been touched did not know. The page says this chain is here for the disclosure failure at least as much as the technical one.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale. Stage 03 gives the times on March 22.

== Okta's initial assessment, and the final report it commissioned
layout: compare
col: Okta's initial assessment | Up to 366 customers may have been affected | Around 2.5% of Okta's base | The page's figure for customers potentially affected
col: The final report Okta commissioned | Conclusions published by Okta in April 2022 | One Sitel workstation controlled for 25 minutes, on January 21 | Two active customer tenants accessed
The page uses 366 for customers potentially affected, and two for tenants actually accessed.
notes:
The page gives two figures, and they answer different questions.

Okta initially assessed that up to 366 customers, around 2.5% of its base, may have been affected. That is a count of customers potentially affected. Keep the words up to and may have been when you say it.

The final forensic report Okta commissioned, whose conclusions Okta published in April 2022, found the attacker had controlled one Sitel workstation for 25 minutes on January 21 and accessed two active customer tenants. A tenant is one customer's own space in a shared platform.

The page also says LAPSUS$ had access to Sitel's environment between January 16 and 21. The 25 minutes is the final report's finding for control of one workstation. The page gives both and does not set them against each other, so do not reconcile them aloud.

The page calls the technical blast radius bounded and Okta's eventual analysis thorough.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | A subcontractor's support laptop | T1199
step: 02 | The Access | Support can reset passwords and MFA | T1098
step: 03 | The Failure | Two months of silence | -
step: 04 | Impact | Two tenants accessed, per the final report | T1213
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stage 03 carries no ATT&CK technique.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's four stages, each with its phase and, where the page gives one, the ATT&CK technique it maps to it.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is LAPSUS$ compromising the laptop of a support engineer at Sitel, a subcontractor providing support services to Okta. Stage 02, which the page calls The Access, is what support tooling can do: reset passwords and multi-factor authentication, or MFA, for customer users. Stage 03, The Failure, is two months of silence, and it carries no ATT&CK technique. Stage 04, Impact, is two tenants accessed, per Okta's final report, up to 366 customers potentially affected, and a lasting question about trust in identity providers.

The page describes the capability support tooling holds at stage 02. It does not say which actions the attacker took with it.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== LAPSUS$ compromised a Sitel support engineer's laptop
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Actor | LAPSUS$ / DEV-0537, known for recruiting insiders and buying access at outsourcers
fact: Visibility | customers had effectively none into Sitel
fact: Why this layer | broad access, weaker controls, less scrutiny than the brand it serves
Exposed organizations were three relationships away from the compromised endpoint.
notes:
Stage 01 is the way in. The page maps it to T1199, Trusted Relationship, and T1078, Valid Accounts.

LAPSUS$ compromised the laptop of a customer support engineer working for Sitel, a subcontractor providing support services to Okta. The page gives the window as January 16 to 21, 2022, five days. It tells you to count the hops, and the next slide draws them.

The organizations ultimately exposed were three relationships away from the compromised endpoint, with no contractual or practical visibility into it. Most would not have known Sitel existed.

The page says LAPSUS$ was known for buying credentials and recruiting insiders at outsourcers and telecoms, precisely because that layer combines broad access with weaker controls and lower scrutiny than the brand-name company it serves. That describes the group's known methods and why it targets this layer. The page does not say which method put LAPSUS$ on this laptop, so do not supply one.

The page's actor line pairs LAPSUS$ with DEV-0537, the name used in Microsoft's analysis, one of the page's sources.

== Count the hops, as the page draws them
layout: layers
layer: Customer | An Okta customer, which trusts Okta
layer: Identity provider | Okta, which outsources customer support to Sitel
layer: Support subcontractor | Sitel, whose staff work on machines Sitel manages
layer: Endpoint | A support engineer's laptop, which LAPSUS$ compromised
Each arrow is one relationship the page states. The organizations ultimately exposed had no contractual or practical visibility into the laptop.
notes:
This slide draws the chain the page gives, top to bottom: an Okta customer, Okta, Sitel, and a support engineer's laptop. Each arrow is one relationship the page states.

An Okta customer trusts Okta. Okta outsources customer support to Sitel. Sitel staff work on machines Sitel manages. LAPSUS$ compromised one of those machines.

So the organizations ultimately exposed were three relationships away from the compromised endpoint, and had no contractual or practical visibility into it. The page tags this stage Fourth Party, and calls the customers' visibility into Sitel effectively none.

One of the page's defenses comes back to this layer: map the fourth party, at least for critical vendors.

The next slide gives ATT&CK's general description of a trusted relationship.

== What ATT&CK calls a trusted relationship
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1199 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1199/
ATT&CK says adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations that have access to intended victims. Such access abuses an existing connection that may not be protected, or that receives less scrutiny than standard ways into a network.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes T1199, Trusted Relationship, as adversaries breaching or otherwise leveraging organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through a trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected, or that receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.

It says organizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers, so that they can manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Its examples include IT services contractors, managed security providers and infrastructure contractors. Valid accounts that the other party uses for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.

ATT&CK's description names Valid Accounts, T1078, which is the second technique the page maps to stage 01.

== Support engineers can reset passwords and MFA for customer users
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: The Access
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: Per Okta, support cannot | create or delete users, download customer databases, access source code
fact: Support can | reset passwords and reset MFA for customer users
fact: Same capability | Scattered Spider / MGM, via a phone call, eighteen months later
The page calls this the precise capability an attacker wants from an IdP, and says a support console there should be treated as a privileged administrative system.
notes:
Stage 02 is what that support access could do. The page maps it to T1098, Account Manipulation.

Okta was clear that support personnel cannot create or delete users, download customer databases, or access source code. But the access they do have, the page says, is exactly the access an attacker wants from an identity provider: the ability to reset a password, and the ability to reset multi-factor authentication for a user. The page calls it limited by design, but powerful by nature.

The page describes this capability. It does not say which actions the attacker took with it.

It is the same capability Scattered Spider obtained by phoning a help desk at MGM eighteen months later. The page says that capability is why a support console at an IdP should be treated as a privileged administrative system rather than a customer service tool.

The screenshots LAPSUS$ published showed internal applications and a Slack channel. The page reads that as a reminder that support tooling tends to sit alongside internal chat, where more context is casually available.

The next slide explains MFA and identity providers in general terms.

== What MFA and an identity provider are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, MFA | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-mfa
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry for IdP (csoh.org)
MFA, multi-factor authentication, is something you know plus something you have, or something you are.

An identity provider, or IdP, is the system that authenticates users.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines MFA, multi-factor authentication, as something you know plus something you have, or something you are.

It defines an identity provider, or IdP, as the system that authenticates users, and gives Okta, Azure AD or Entra ID, and Google Workspace as examples.

The page's stage 02 concerns resetting a password and resetting MFA for a user, at an identity provider.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-idp

== Sitel's summary reached Okta March 17; customers learned March 22
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: The Failure
fact: Customers learned | from screenshots LAPSUS$ posted to Telegram
fact: The two months | spent waiting on a subcontractor's investigation
fact: Net effect | customers could not check their own logs during the window it mattered
fact: The delay | a mistake, Okta's chief security officer later acknowledged
The page's lesson: a contractual notification obligation which permits a two-month forensic process is not a security control.
notes:
Stage 03 is the reason the page includes this chain. It carries no ATT&CK technique.

The page calls the published timeline unusually precise and unusually damning. Okta received a summary report from Sitel on March 17. LAPSUS$ posted screenshots at 03:30 UTC on March 22. By 05:00 UTC, Okta Security had determined they related to the January Sitel incident, and Okta received the complete forensic report from Sitel at 12:27 UTC that same day.

So, the page says, the correlation took ninety minutes once the evidence was public, while the two months before it were spent waiting on a subcontractor's investigation.

The customers who might have needed to check their own logs for anomalous password or MFA resets in January were not asked to, because nobody told them. Okta's chief security officer subsequently acknowledged the delay was a mistake.

The page's general lesson is that a contractual notification obligation which permits a two-month forensic process is not a security control. Your customers' ability to respond decays to nothing long before the report is finished.

The next slide sets out March 22 by the clock.

== March 22, 2022, by the clock
layout: timeline
event: 03:30 UTC | LAPSUS$ publishes screenshots
event: By 05:00 UTC | Okta Security has tied them to the January Sitel incident
event: 12:27 UTC | Okta receives the complete forensic report from Sitel
Times in UTC. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
This is March 22, 2022, using the times the page gives.

LAPSUS$ posted screenshots at 03:30 UTC. By 05:00 UTC, Okta Security had determined they related to the January Sitel incident. That is the ninety minutes. Okta received the complete forensic report from Sitel at 12:27 UTC the same day.

Set that against the calendar. Okta had received a summary report from Sitel on March 17, and the intrusion was in January. Correlation took ninety minutes once the evidence was public. The two months before it were spent waiting on a subcontractor's investigation.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Two tenants accessed, per Okta's final report
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Impact
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: Okta first assessed | up to 366 customers, ~2.5% of its base, may have been affected
fact: Final report found | two active customer tenants accessed, during 25 minutes of control on January 21
fact: Technical scope | bounded by what support tooling permits
fact: Recurrence | Okta's support breached again in October 2023, by another route
An identity provider's supply chain is your supply chain, the page says.
notes:
Stage 04 is the impact. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories.

Okta first assessed that up to 366 customers, roughly 2.5% of its base, may have been affected. Its final investigation found two active customer tenants accessed, during 25 minutes of control on January 21. The page says the technical blast radius was bounded and Okta's eventual analysis was thorough. It gives the technical scope as bounded by what support tooling permits.

The page says the reputational and strategic effect was larger, because it forced a question the industry had been avoiding: an identity provider is a single point of trust for every application behind it, so its supply chain is your supply chain, and its subcontractors are your subcontractors. It says the incident permanently changed how organizations reason about identity provider concentration.

Okta was breached again through its support organization in October 2023, by a different route, and that chain sits on the site separately. The page says the recurrence is the point. LAPSUS$ itself was disrupted later in 2022, following arrests in the UK.

The next slide explains tenants and blast radius in general terms.

== What a tenant and blast radius are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Tenant | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-tenant
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry for Blast Radius (csoh.org)
A tenant is a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform.

Blast radius is the scope of damage if a credential, identity or system is compromised.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines a tenant as a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform. Multi-tenant software as a service keeps many tenants isolated on the same infrastructure.

It defines blast radius as the scope of damage if a credential, identity or system is compromised, and calls minimizing it a design principle.

The page uses both words at stage 04: two active customer tenants accessed, and a technical blast radius it calls bounded.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-blast-radius

== A fairly ordinary intrusion, and a defining disclosure failure
layout: text
The page calls the intrusion a fairly ordinary third-party compromise. The disclosure made it a defining incident.
- Correlation took ninety minutes once the evidence was public.
- The two months before it were spent waiting on a subcontractor's investigation.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws in its summary.

The intrusion itself is a fairly ordinary third-party compromise. What made this a defining incident for the industry was the disclosure. For two months, the organizations whose identity provider had been touched did not know, and the page says the chain is here for the disclosure failure at least as much as the technical one.

The contrast is in the timeline. Correlation took ninety minutes once the evidence was public. The two months before it were spent waiting on a subcontractor's investigation.

The page adds a second point at stage 04. Okta was breached again through its support organization in October 2023, by a different route, and the page says the recurrence is the point.

The defenses that follow start with notification and with the case where the vendor is not the one telling you.

== Five defenses against four stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04
row: Notification timelines in hours, in contracts | 03
row: Ask who else can reset your users' MFA | 02
row: Monitor your tenant for support-console actions | 02 | 03
row: Map the fourth party for critical vendors | 01
row: Plan for a vendor that is not telling you | 03
No defense names the two tenants Okta's final report found accessed, or the 366. Two name your tenant: restricting or approving support access to it, and monitoring it.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's five defenses against its four stages. A cell is marked where a defense names a detail of that stage's own events. The page itself ties no defense to a stage by number.

Notification timelines answer stage 03's two-month wait for a complete report. Their word subcontractors is shared by stages 01 and 03, so column 01 is not marked.

Asking who else can reset your users' MFA names stage 02's capability and support console. Its word outsourcers appears in stage 01 only in passing, so column 01 is not marked.

Monitoring your own tenant names stage 02's resets, and stage 03's customers who were not asked to check their own logs.

Mapping the fourth party names stage 01's exposed endpoint: a support engineer's laptop that, Okta says, was owned and managed by Sitel.

Planning for a vendor that is not telling you names stage 03's two months, which it says waiting for confirmation cost.

Column 04 is empty: no defense names the two tenants, the 366 or identity provider concentration. Two defenses name your tenant, restricting or approving support access to it and monitoring it, but neither names the two tenants accessed. Whether they reach stage 04 is the second discussion question.

== Notification in hours, and a plan for when nobody tells you
layout: compare
col: Contract timelines (stage 03) | Notice of suspected compromise within a defined short window, in hours | Required on suspicion, before forensics conclude | Extended explicitly to their subcontractors
col: When the vendor is not telling you (stage 03) | Decide how you validate a public claim about a vendor | Name who owns acting on unconfirmed reporting | Choose your pre-emptive defensive steps in advance
The page says waiting for confirmation cost these customers two months.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 03.

First, write notification timelines into contracts in hours, not "promptly". The page calls this the concrete, transferable fix. Require vendors to notify you of suspected compromise affecting your data within a defined short window, and specifically require notification on suspicion, before forensics conclude, because the two-month gap here was spent waiting for a complete report. Extend the requirement explicitly to their subcontractors.

Second, plan for the incident where the vendor is not the one telling you. Here the notification was effectively a Telegram post. Decide in advance how you validate a public claim about a vendor, who owns the decision to act on unconfirmed reporting, and what defensive steps you take pre-emptively, because waiting for confirmation cost these customers two months.

The first discussion question comes back to the second of these.

== Ask who can reset MFA, and watch your tenant
layout: compare
col: Ask your IdP (stage 02) | Who, at the vendor and its outsourcers, can reset your users' MFA? | What controls sit around that console? | Are actions logged in a way you can consume? | Can you restrict or approve support access to your tenant?
col: Monitor your tenant (stages 02, 03) | Watch password resets, MFA re-enrolments and privilege changes | Alert on them as security events rather than help-desk noise | Retain the logs yourself
The page says several IdPs now let you restrict or approve support access.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 02, and the second reaches stage 03 as well.

First, ask your identity provider who else can reset your users' MFA. Support staff, at the vendor and at its outsourcers, hold that capability. Find out who they are, what controls sit around that console, whether actions are logged in a way you can consume, and whether you can restrict or approve support access to your tenant. The page says several IdPs now offer exactly that control because of this incident.

Second, monitor your own tenant for the actions a compromised support console would produce. You cannot see inside the vendor, but you can see the outcome: password resets, MFA re-enrolments, and privilege changes on your users. Alert on those as security events rather than help-desk noise, and retain the logs yourself. Then, the page says, you retain the ability to answer the question even when nobody tells you to ask it.

That is stage 03's gap, where customers were not asked to check their own logs, because nobody told them.

== Map the fourth party, at least for critical vendors
layout: text
The exposed laptop was owned and managed by Sitel, Okta says.
- Ask critical vendors who they outsource to.
- Ask what access those parties hold, and how they are assessed.
- For an IdP, know one level further than the contract.
notes:
This defense answers stage 01.

The exposed endpoint was a support engineer's laptop that, Okta says, was owned and managed by Sitel, an Okta sub-processor through its acquisition of Sykes. A sub-processor is an outside company that handles data on another company's behalf. For the handful of vendors that could hurt you most, the page says, ask who they outsource to, what access those parties hold, and how they are assessed.

The page sets a limit. You will not map the whole chain, but for an identity provider it is worth knowing one level further than the contract.

Stage 01 shows why. The organizations ultimately exposed were three relationships away from the compromised endpoint, with no contractual or practical visibility into it, and most would not have known Sitel existed.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Do your vendor contracts require notice in hours, on suspicion, before forensics conclude?
check: Do those terms extend explicitly to your vendors' subcontractors?
check: Who at your IdP, and at its outsourcers, can reset your users' MFA?
check: Can you restrict or approve support access to your tenant?
check: Do password resets, MFA re-enrolments and privilege changes alert, with logs you retain?
check: Who do your critical vendors outsource to, and what access do those parties hold?
check: Have you decided who acts on an unconfirmed public claim about a vendor?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your vendor contracts set notification timelines in hours rather than "promptly", and require notice on suspicion, before forensics conclude. Ask whether that requirement extends explicitly to your vendors' subcontractors.

Ask who, at your identity provider and at its outsourcers, can reset your users' MFA, what controls sit around that console, and whether you can restrict or approve support access to your tenant.

Ask whether password resets, MFA re-enrolments and privilege changes on your users alert as security events, and whether you retain those logs yourself.

Ask who your most critical vendors outsource to, what access those parties hold, and how they are assessed.

Finally, ask whether you have decided in advance how you validate a public claim about a vendor, and who owns the decision to act on unconfirmed reporting.

== If the first notice is a public post, who acts?
layout: question
Here the notification was effectively a Telegram post, and the page says waiting for confirmation cost these customers two months.

Suppose screenshots appear claiming access to one of your critical vendors. Who decides what you do first?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page says the notification here was effectively a Telegram post. Customers found out on March 22, when LAPSUS$ posted screenshots, and Okta correlated them to the January incident within ninety minutes of seeing them.

Ask each person to name who in their organization would make the call, and what they would do before the vendor confirmed anything. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says to decide in advance.

== What the page says to decide in advance
layout: answer
Plan for the incident where the vendor is not the one telling you. Decide in advance:
- How you validate a public claim about a vendor.
- Who owns the decision to act on unconfirmed reporting.
- What defensive steps you take pre-emptively.
notes:
The page's answer is to decide in advance.

Decide how you validate a public claim about a vendor, who owns the decision to act on unconfirmed reporting, and what defensive steps you take pre-emptively, because waiting for confirmation cost these customers two months.

Another of the page's defenses speaks to the case where nobody tells you. Monitor your own tenant for the outcome a compromised support console would produce: password resets, MFA re-enrolments, and privilege changes on your users. Alert on those as security events and retain the logs yourself, and the page says you retain the ability to answer the question even when nobody tells you to ask it.

Bring the group's answers back to this. If nobody owns the decision to act on unconfirmed reporting, the page's point applies: waiting for confirmation cost these customers two months.

== Do any defenses reach stage 04?
layout: question
No defense names the two tenants Okta's final report found accessed, or the 366. Two name your tenant: restricting or approving support access to it, and monitoring it.

Do those reach stage 04? What would you add?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in the defense matrix.

This deck's matrix ties the page's defenses to stages 01, 02 and 03. None of them names what stage 04 reports: Okta's initial assessment of up to 366 customers potentially affected, the two active customer tenants its final report found accessed, or the point the page draws from them, that an identity provider is a single point of trust for every application behind it.

Two defenses come close, because they name your tenant. One asks whether you can restrict or approve support access to your tenant. The other says to monitor your own tenant for the actions a compromised support console would produce.

Let the group decide whether those reach stage 04 before you show the answer slide, and ask each new proposal to name the detail from stage 04 that it would address.

== No defense is marked for stage 04; two come close
layout: answer
This deck's matrix ties no defense to stage 04, and the deck adds none. Two defenses name your tenant: restrict or approve support access to it, and monitor it. Whether they reach stage 04 is the group's call.
notes:
Be clear about where the deck stops. Its matrix ties no defense to stage 04, because no defense names the two tenants, the 366 or identity provider concentration, and the deck adds none of its own. The page itself ties no defense to a stage by number.

Two of the page's defenses name your tenant. One asks whether you can restrict or approve support access to your tenant, and says several IdPs now offer exactly that control because of this incident. The other says to monitor your own tenant for the actions a compromised support console would produce. A third, mapping the fourth party, says that for an identity provider it is worth knowing one level further than the contract. Let the group weigh whether each reaches stage 04; that judgement is the group's own.

Stage 04 draws its own lesson: an identity provider's supply chain is your supply chain, and its subcontractors are your subcontractors.

Test every idea against the facts the page reports for stage 04: up to 366 customers potentially affected in Okta's initial assessment, two active customer tenants accessed during 25 minutes of control on January 21, per the final report, and a technical scope bounded by what support tooling permits.

